<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Quang Trung</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1753–<a href="/years/1792">1792</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Nguyen Hue, Nguyễn Huệ · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tay-son-dynasty">Tây Sơn dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Quang Trung, born Nguyen Hue, was the ablest of the three Tay Son brothers who rose against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nguyen-lords">Nguyen lords</a> in 1771. He overthrew the Trinh lords in 1786, proclaimed himself emperor in 1788 and routed a Qing invasion at Dong Da in 1789. He began land and administrative reforms but died in 1792.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Nguyễn Huệ was born in 1753 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tay-son-dynasty">Tây Sơn</a> village, in Quy Nhơn Province, then in the south of the country. His ancestors were peasants from Nghệ An, and his birth name was Hồ Thơm. His father had eight children, three of whom took part in the Tây Sơn Rebellion: Nguyễn Nhạc, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nguyen-lu">Nguyễn Lữ</a> and Huệ.</p><p>After two centuries of rule, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nguyen-lords">Nguyễn lords</a> had weakened, and after the death of Lord Nguyễn Phúc Khoát the official Trương Phúc Loan took control of the government. The brothers gathered an army and revolted, and quickly occupied the central part of the Nguyễn territory from Quy Nhơn to Bình Thuận. In 1776 Nhạc proclaimed himself King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tay-son-dynasty">Tây Sơn</a> and gave Huệ the title Phụ Chính. In 1777 Huệ and Lữ captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ho-chi-minh-city">Saigon</a> and destroyed the Nguyễn army, killing most of the family except <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gia-long">Nguyễn Ánh</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>In 1786 an army under Huệ marched north, took Phú Xuân and then <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thang-long">Thăng Long</a>. Lê Hiển Tông welcomed him, gave him the title of supreme commander and a daughter, Lê Ngọc Hân, in marriage. When Nhạc proclaimed himself Central Emperor, the brothers fell into civil war, which ended with a boundary agreed at Bến Bản.</p><p>In late 1788 two Chinese armies invaded to restore the Lê emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/le-chieu-thong">Lê Chiêu Thống</a>, at the wish of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qianlong-emperor">Qianlong Emperor</a>. Huệ then sought to restore the tributary relationship, writing as Nguyễn Quang Bình, and in 1789 Qianlong made him King of Annam, on condition that he lead a delegation to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a>; Chinese and Vietnamese sources agree that he sent an imposter. He nevertheless regarded himself as emperor of Đại Việt. On the advice of Nguyễn Thiếp he began a new capital at Phượng Hoàng Trung Đô in Nghệ An and renamed Thăng Long Bắc Thành.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Quang Trung fell ill suddenly and died on 16 September 1792, at about 40. The official records do not name the illness, and historians think a stroke possible. On his deathbed he called his heir <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canh-thinh">Nguyễn Quang Toản</a> a clever boy but too young. Toản did not continue his policies, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gia-long">Nguyễn Ánh</a> overthrew the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tay-son-dynasty">Tây Sơn</a> in 1802, razing the tombs of Nhạc and Huệ.</p><p>Quang Trung came to be regarded as a national saviour and one of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a>'s most popular figures; most cities have named major streets after him, and a museum in his hometown shows documents and artifacts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tay-son-dynasty">Tây Sơn</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Quang Trung” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Tây Sơn rebellion, which broke out in the Nguyễn domain in 1771, destroyed the Nguyễn lords, and in 1786 its leader <b>Nguyễn Huệ</b> marched north and overthrew the Trịnh while professing loyalty to the Lê.</blockquote><a href="/empire/later-le-dynasty">Later Le dynasty · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The teenage Nguyen Anh survived and spent more than two decades fighting the Tay Son from bases in the Mekong delta, at times taking refuge in Siam, whose army was defeated on his behalf by the Tay Son leader <b>Nguyen Hue</b> in 1785.</blockquote><a href="/empire/nguyen-dynasty">Nguyen dynasty · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1771 the brothers Nguyen Nhac, <b>Nguyen Hue</b> and Nguyen Lu of Tay Son village in Binh Dinh rose against the Nguyen lords, attracting peasants, merchants and highland peoples with promises to redistribute wealth.</blockquote><a href="/state/tay-son-dynasty">Tay Son dynasty</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1786 the Tay Son under <b>Nguyen Hue</b> marched on Thang Long and defeated Trinh Khai, and the last lord, Trinh Bong, fled in 1787.</blockquote><a href="/state/trinh-lords">Trinh lords</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/binh-dinh-province">Bình Định</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/vietnam">Vietnam</a>. Died in Huế, in what is now <a href="/countries/vietnam">Vietnam</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=14.17,109&amp;year=1778" aria-label="Bình Định on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7534 1636 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Quang Trung"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7984 1906h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7984 1906h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=14.17,109&amp;year=1778">What was here in 1778? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
